### Why is idempotency non-negotiable during migration?

During the transition, dual publication or redelivery can cause the same operation to arrive twice. The consumer must deduplicate by message ID.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class IdempotencyDuringMigration {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Why idempotency is critical during migration:");
        System.out.println("  1. Outbox relay may publish same event twice (at-least-once)");
        System.out.println("  2. Consumer may crash and get redelivery");
        System.out.println("  3. Both REST and Kafka paths may process briefly");
        System.out.println("  -> Without idempotency: Rajesh charged twice");
        System.out.println("  -> With idempotency: second attempt is a no-op");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Why idempotency is critical during migration:
  1. Outbox relay may publish same event twice (at-least-once)
  2. Consumer may crash and get redelivery
  3. Both REST and Kafka paths may process briefly
  -> Without idempotency: Rajesh charged twice
  -> With idempotency: second attempt is a no-op
```

### What are the safety mechanisms at each stage?

| Stage | Authoritative | Validation | Rollback |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1. Coexistence | REST | None yet | Remove outbox |
| 2. Shadow | REST | Reconciliation vs shadow | Remove shadow consumer |
| 3. Feature flag | Per-account | Reconciliation + monitoring | Flip flag back |
| 4. Decommission | Kafka | Monitoring only | Redeploy old code (emergency) |

```text
Safety guarantees:
  - Idempotency: no duplication at any stage
  - Outbox: no event loss
  - Reconciliation: proves equivalence
  - Feature flag: instant rollback control surface
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The feature flag is the control surface, not the safety mechanism. Idempotency prevents duplication, the outbox prevents loss, and reconciliation gives confidence to advance.

```text
Senior insights:
  - Reconciliation must compare at the BALANCE level, not just event counts
  - Feature flag should be deterministic (by account_id hash, not random)
  - Monitor consumer lag, error rate, DLQ volume at each ramp step
  - Keep rollback capability at every stage
  - This approach works for any sync-to-async migration, not just REST-to-Kafka
```
